227, 0.08584135299997797, 0.08622260999999298, 0.11203487199998108, 0.16849067800001194, 0.25782218300003024, 0.08718502299996089, 0.08681115500002079, 0.08687236299999768, 0.08645628699997587, 0.08640260500004615, 0.08599767799995561, 0.08641635100002532, 0.2606432040000186, 0.08774084199995968, 0.0869278940000413, 2.3098999974990875e-05]], "generated_texts": ["Yes, I am very familiar with **\"Traction: Get a Grip on Your Business\"** by Gino Wickman.\n\nIt is one of the most popular business management books because it focuses on a single, cohesive system rather than a collection of scattered tips. The core of the book is the **Entrepreneurial Operating System (EOS)**.\n\nHere is a high-level summary of the key concepts to help you get oriented:\n\n### 1. The Core Philosophy\nWickman argues that every business owner faces the same frustrations: lack of control, people/process issues,", "To create this application, we will use three main components:\n1.  **`arboard`**: To handle cross-platform clipboard access.\n2.  **`reqwest`**: To send data to the ElevenLabs API.\n3.  **`rodio`**: To play the audio bytes received from the API.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nYou must have an **ElevenLabs API Key**. You can find this in your [ElevenLabs Dashboard](https://elevenlabs.io/app/subscriptions).\n\n### Step 1: Create the Project\n\nOpen your terminal and run:\n\n```bash\ncargo new rust-tts-clipboard\ncd rust-tts-clipboard\n```\n\n### Step 2: Add Dependencies\n\nOpen `Cargo.toml` and add these dependencies:\n\n```toml\n[dependencies]\nreqwest = { version = \"0.12\", features = [\"multipart\", \"json\"] }\ntokio = { version = \"1\", features = [\"full\"] }\narboard = \"0.15\"\nrodio = \"0.17\"\nserde = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"derive\"] }\nserde_json = \"1.0\"\n```\n\n### Step 3: The Code\n\nReplace the contents of `src/main.rs` with the following code. I have added comments to explain each section.\n\n```rust\nuse arboard::Clipboard;\nuse rodio::{Decoder, OutputStream};\nuse std::io::Cursor;\nuse std::time::Duration;\nuse tokio::time::sleep;\n\n// Replace this with your actual API Key\nconst ELEVENLABS_API_KEY: &str = \"YOUR_API_KEY_HERE\";\n// You can find your Voice ID in the ElevenLabs dashboard (e.ties/voice settings)\nconst VOICE_ID: &str = \"21m00Tcm4TlvDq98678a\"; \n\n#[tokio::main]\nasync fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n    println!(\"--- Clipboard TTS Runner ---\");\n    println!(\"Monitoring clipboard... (Press Ctrl+C to stop)\");\n\n    let mut clipboard = Clipboard::new()?;\n    let mut last_text = String::new();\n\n    // We use a loop to poll the clipboard. \n    // In a production app, you might use a timer or OS-specific hooks.\n    loop {\n        if let Ok(text) = clipboard.get_text() {\n            // Only trigger if the text has changed and isn't empty\n            if text != last_text && !text.is_empty() {\n                println!(\"\\nNew text detected: '{}...'\", &text[..text.len().min(30)]);\n                \n                // Process TTS\n                match text_to_speech(&text).await {\n                    Ok(audio_data) => {\n                        play_audio(audio_data)?;\n                    }\n                    Err(e) => eprintln!(\"Error: {}\", e),\n                }\n                \n                last_text = text;\n            }\n        }\n\n        // Wait 2 seconds before checking again to avoid spamming the API\n        sleep(Duration::from_secs(2)).await;\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn text_to_speech(text: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n    let url = format!(\"https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech/{}\", VOICE_ID);\n    \n    // Prepare the JSON payload\n    let payload = serde_json::json!({\n        \"text\": text,\n        \"model_id\": \"eleven_monolingual_v1\",\n        \"voice_settings\": {\n            \"stability\": 0.5,\n            \"similarity_boost\": 0.5\n        }", "Since you didn't provide the original text, I have created a template for your **\"Second Draft.\"** \n\nTo make a \"Filters\" section easy to read, I have applied three professional UX/UI writing principles:\n1.  **Categorization:** Grouping related filters so the user doesn't have to hunt.\n2.  **Visual Hierarchy:** Using bold headers and clear spacing.\n3.  **Clean Syntax:** Using \"Type > Attribute\" logic (e.g., *Size > XL*) to reduce wordiness.\n\nHere are two ways to reformat it. Choose the one that fits your project best.\n\n***\n\n# Second Draft\n\n## [Project Name/Title]\n\n### 1. Overview\n[Insert your introductory text here...]\n\n### 2. Filters\n*I have reorganized these into logical groups to prevent \"choice paralysis.\"*\n\n**[Category A: e.g., Product Details]**\n* **Type:** [Option 1] | [Option 2] | [Option 3]\n* **Size/Scale:** [Option 1] | [Option 2] | [Option 3]\n* **Color/Style:** [Option 1]", "In the context of **Jobs-to-be-Done (JTBD)**, a **\"Push\"** refers to the internal or external forces that create dissatisfaction with a user's current situation, driving them to seek change.\n\nTo understand \"Push,\" it is best to look at it as part of a framework often used in JTBD called the **Four Forces Model** (developed by Bob Moesi). This model explains why people \"hire\" a new solution.\n\n### The Four Forces Framework\nTo understand where \"Push\" fits, you have to see the interplay between all four forces:\n\n1.  **Push (The Current Situation):** Problems, frustrations, or inefficiencies with the current way of doing things.\n2.  **Pull (The New Solution):** The attraction toward a new alternative (the promise of the new way).\n3.  **Anxiety (The Risk of Change):** The fear of the unknown", "It looks like you have provided a segment of a **Project Charter** for a web-based commercial appraisal application. To make this document complete and professional, you are currently missing the \"top half\" of the charter (the context) and the specific \"Risk\" headers.\n\nBelow is a structured template based on the content you provided. I have organized it into a logical flow and added placeholders for the missing sections to help you complete the document.\n\n---\n\n# Project Charter: [Insert Project Name]\n\n## \u8a33 1"], "errors": ["", "", "", "", ""], "mean_ttft_ms": 2409.539446999986, "median_ttft_ms": 2412.180572000011, "std_ttft_ms": 57.01077238001846, "p99_ttft_ms": 2492.2893400799876, "mean_tpot_ms": 72.79319965397723, "median_tpot_ms": 75.74362764062492, "std_tpot_ms": 12.500087431736048, "p99_tpot_ms": 84.25199006397662, "mean_itl_ms": 71.28242193992396, "median_itl_ms": 63.84588700001359, "std_itl_ms": 36.601117177855386, "p99_itl_ms": 255.28219547999808}